WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.751
(tank blasting)

00:00:02.751 --> 00:00:03.990
- [Narrator] The M1 Abrams tank

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.000
is one of the most powerful ground weapons

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.350
in the U.S. arsenal

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.290
equipped with a 1500
horsepower turbine engine

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.470
and a top speed of more
than 40 miles per hour.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:17.910
It comes with a 120 millimeter
cannon, three machine guns,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.120
and armor that can withstand
frontal hits from enemy tanks

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.410
with minimal damage.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.810
And these tanks could
help Ukrainian forces

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.333
take back Russian occupied territories.

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:28.920
(tank blasting)

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.500
But Pentagon officials
have expressed concerns

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.230
that these tanks could pose
major logistical issues

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.190
for Ukraine.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.110
- The Abrams requires a lot of support,

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.540
and if the Ukrainians don't
have everything that it takes

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.640
to operate the Abrams, they might end up

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.980
being more of a detriment
than they are a help.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.260
- [Narrator] If Ukrainians
want to take full advantage

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.850
of the M1 Abrams tanks,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.793
they'll have to solve several
major logistical challenges.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.250
- So this binder right here,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.990
this contains all the information
and coordinating details

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.020
to operate a battalion of Abrams tanks

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.660
for four days in the field.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.880
- [Narrator] Dan Grazier
is a Marine Corps veteran

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.610
who operated Abrams tanks
for almost a decade.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.910
- Old Western tanks are a lot more complex

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.250
than the old Soviet tanks.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.500
There's a lot of upgraded electronics.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.630
They're much more intricately designed.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.040
- [Narrator] Ukraine already has

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.650
a lot of skilled tank
operators who have experience

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.820
working on older Soviet
era tanks, like T-72s.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:27.653
- You got to remember

00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:30.240
they've been fighting
the Russians since 2014,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.000
so they are not gonna
be starting from scratch

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.680
learning how to drive a tank here.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.380
They will have to learn how
to use the Abrams tanks.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.060
They'll have to learn
how to use the, you know,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.470
the targeting system.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.330
The Abrams is a four-person tank.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.970
The Soviet tanks have
three people in them.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.490
So they'll have to learn details,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.560
but if you know how to drive a tank,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:50.730
you know how to drive a tank.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.260
So one way to think about this

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:57.260
would be you go from a
10-year-old Toyota to a new Tesla.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:58.950
You know how to drive.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.800
You just have to learn how
to do it on this new vehicle.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.420
- [Narrator] These new Abrams tanks

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.310
boast impressive technology.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.740
The tech enables troops to fire at night

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.290
from longer ranges than Russian tanks,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.810
navigate through waters
up to four feet deep,

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.900
and shoot rounds with a range
of two and a half miles.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.060
But the more complicated the technology,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.920
the more likely it is to break.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.020
And depending on what
needs to be repaired,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.390
the parts may need to
be sent all the way back

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.790
to manufacturing plants in the U.S.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.560
Then after they're repaired,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.750
they'll need to be sent back to Ukraine.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.900
- A lot of the individual
components inside the tank

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.590
were designed not necessarily
to be repaired in the field,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.210
but to be replaced in the field.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.450
It could be days or
possibly even weeks for them

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.130
to get a replacement component.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.510
- [Narrator] And if a tank has to wait

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.980
on a replacement part?

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.440
- If the tank doesn't get
everything that it needs

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.800
in terms of spare parts, of fuel,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.640
of even lubricants and hydraulic fluid,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.980
you only have to lose one
or two of those things

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.100
to render a tank ineffective
and it becomes little more

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.700
than a giant 72 ton
paperweight on the battlefield.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:06.540
- [Narrator] And one of the most

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.160
logistically difficult parts

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.490
for maintaining an M1
Abrams tank is fueling it.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.910
- The reluctance within the Pentagon

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.520
to sending the Abrams
tanks had a lot to do

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.140
with how difficult it is
to keep them in the fight.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.730
- Abrams tanks use a lot of gas

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.100
because they're very large heavy vehicles,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.110
so it takes a great deal of energy

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.080
to be able to even get
that thing to start moving.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.400
- [Narrator] In order to avoid the risk

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.690
of running out of fuel,

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.450
Abrams tanks typically
travel with fuel trucks.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.450
The tanks can store
about 500 gallons of fuel

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.990
and can go about 275
miles on a single fill up.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.580
And while it takes a lot of gas

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.560
to start and run an Abrams tank,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.250
it also consumes a huge amount
of fuel even when it's idle.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.470
- Just the capacity is
gonna be the big challenge.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.270
Just making sure that
they have enough fuel

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.690
to power these tanks, that
might be a challenge for them.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.070
Tanks are only one part
of the overall equation.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.110
The tanks require a lot of support.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.390
So they require refueler vehicles,

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.340
they require maintenance vehicles,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.550
they require ammunition trucks,
you know, to support them.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.960
And if you take those out,

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.570
if you're really able to
disrupt that supply chain,

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:18.750
then the tanks are
irrelevant at that point.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.100
- [Narrator] Tanks are able to get through

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.220
a variety of terrains,
but tank support vehicles

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.680
can have a difficult time
traveling through mud,

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.990
snow, and other rough terrains.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.870
- If conditions degrade,
you know, bridges are out,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.150
excessive mud, deep snow,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.720
that can all disrupt the supply chain

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.360
and make it a lot harder
to properly refuel

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.670
and re-arm those tanks.
- [Narrator] And for the tanks

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.710
to operate the most effectively,

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.480
Ukrainian forces will need to manage

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.970
a pipeline of high quality fuels.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.060
- The turbine engine in the
Abrams is a multi-fuel engine.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.750
The efficiency and the
effectiveness of the, you know,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.220
of the engine would be reduced

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.257
if you're using kind
of a lower grade fuel.

00:04:58.257 --> 00:05:00.780
The kind of diesel fuel
that the military uses

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.790
will work quite well in the Abrams.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.090
And as long as the
Ukrainians have have access

00:05:05.931 --> 00:05:07.380
to quality diesel fuel,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.050
they should be able to
operate the Abrams just fine.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.760
- [Narrator] But the U.S.'s Abrams tanks

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.670
aren't the only tanks that
Ukraine needs to plan for.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.500
It will need to manage supply chains

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.810
to support multiple types of tanks,

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.110
including its existing Soviet era T-72s,

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.140
Britain's Challenger
2s, Germany's Leopards,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.780
and the U.S.'s M1 Abrams tanks.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.550
And while other tanks may not require

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.260
as much fuel as the Abrams,

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.120
Grazier says Britain's Challenger 2

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.330
and Germany's Leopard are
also technically more complex

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.070
than Ukraine's current tanks.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.710
- There are gonna be some
differences in the details

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.553
as far as how they're supported.
And I think that's gonna be

00:05:43.553 --> 00:05:45.120
a big challenge for the Ukrainians

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.940
in having to maintain three
separate supply chains

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.850
to be able to support
these three different tanks

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.860
that they're going to be receiving there

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.480
over the next couple of months.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.800
- [Narrator] So if Ukrainian forces

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.350
will need to navigate
complicated technology,

00:05:58.350 --> 00:05:59.700
gas guzzling engines,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.130
and create multiple complex supply chains,

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.980
why did the U.S. decide
to send the Abrams tank?

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.680
- Ukraine needs foreign tanks
because the ones they have

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.570
keep getting blown up by the Russians.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.270
They need more tanks, and the
Abrams tank is a good one.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.960
There aren't that many
old Soviet tanks around

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.540
for Ukraine to go shopping for.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.190
- It was designed for the Cold War

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.890
to fight the Soviet
Union in Eastern Europe.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:27.890
So for the Abrams to go to
Ukraine now is, in a way,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.670
almost like it's going home.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.390
- Right now, what Ukraine needs
is offensive capabilities.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:36.630
Not so much defensive to
protect their territory,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.670
but offensive to enable them

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:42.030
to drive the Russians out of their turf.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.670
If you wanna take back
ground in a ground war,

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.051
you need that kind of
heavy armor to do it.

00:06:47.051 --> 00:06:50.301
(tank engine rumbling)

